  "result": "I've saved a short note about your question, and now I'm writing your answer.\n\nReading that RL is used to train strong agents doesn't mean you need to train a model to get better tool calling in your assistant. Training and its math are also outside your mission, so start by measuring what goes wrong.\n\nPPO's core objective, stated rather than derived, is to maximize:\n\n**E[ min( r·A, clip(r, 1−ε, 1+ε)·A ) ]**\n\n`r` is how much more or less likely an action is than before, `A` is how much better than expected it turned out, and clipping removes the reward for pushing `r` too far in one update. Deriving it requires expectations, gradients, neural network training and policy gradients. Using it also requires a model you can update, many scored attempts and computing power.\n\nPicture a new server at SUR from *Vanderpump Rules* getting orders wrong. Before ordering weeks of retraining, you'd check *which* orders fail and why. (Unlike a server, a model doesn't learn just because you corrected it in an earlier request.)\n\n```\n20 test student requests\n        ↓\nrecord each tool call\n        ↓\ncompare with what you expected\n        ↓\ngroup failures → fix one cause → retest\n```\n\nI can make our next lesson about this test-and-fix cycle.\n\n**Your turn:** if 3 of the 20 fail, what would you want to know about them before choosing a fix?",
